Derived query methods parse the method name into a JPQL query. Spring Data follows strict naming conventions:

```java
// Method name → generated JPQL:
List<Trade> findByStatus(String status);
// → SELECT t FROM Trade t WHERE t.status = ?1

List<Trade> findByAccountIdAndStatus(String accountId, String status);
// → SELECT t FROM Trade t WHERE t.accountId = ?1 AND t.status = ?2

List<Trade> findByAmountGreaterThan(BigDecimal amount);
// → SELECT t FROM Trade t WHERE t.amount > ?1

List<Trade> findBySymbolContainingIgnoreCase(String fragment);
// → SELECT t FROM Trade t WHERE UPPER(t.symbol) LIKE UPPER('%' || ?1 || '%')

List<Trade> findByStatusOrderByAmountDesc(String status);
// → SELECT t FROM Trade t WHERE t.status = ?1 ORDER BY t.amount DESC

long countByStatus(String status);
// → SELECT COUNT(t) FROM Trade t WHERE t.status = ?1

boolean existsByAccountIdAndStatus(String accountId, String status);
// → SELECT CASE WHEN COUNT(t) > 0 THEN TRUE ELSE FALSE END ...

void deleteByStatus(String status);
// → DELETE FROM Trade t WHERE t.status = ?1
```

JPQL @Query for complex queries that derived methods can't express:

```java
// Named parameters with @Param:
@Query("SELECT t FROM Trade t WHERE t.amount BETWEEN :min AND :max AND t.status = :status")
List<Trade> findByAmountRangeAndStatus(@Param("min") BigDecimal min,
                                       @Param("max") BigDecimal max,
                                       @Param("status") String status);

// Positional parameters (?1, ?2):
@Query("SELECT t FROM Trade t WHERE t.amount > ?1 AND t.accountId = ?2")
List<Trade> findLargeTradesForAccount(BigDecimal minAmount, String accountId);

// JOIN FETCH (eager load association):
@Query("SELECT a FROM Account a LEFT JOIN FETCH a.trades WHERE a.accountId = :id")
Optional<Account> findAccountWithTrades(@Param("id") String accountId);

// Aggregation:
@Query("SELECT t.symbol, SUM(t.amount) FROM Trade t GROUP BY t.symbol ORDER BY SUM(t.amount) DESC")
List<Object[]> getTotalAmountPerSymbol();
```

Native SQL for database-specific features:

```java
// Native query — raw SQL, uses table/column names not entity/field names:
@Query(value = "SELECT * FROM trades WHERE amount > :min AND settlement_date = CURRENT_DATE",
       nativeQuery = true)
List<Trade> findTodaysLargeTradesNative(@Param("min") BigDecimal min);

// Native with pagination:
@Query(value = "SELECT * FROM trades WHERE status = :status",
       countQuery = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM trades WHERE status = :status",
       nativeQuery = true)
Page<Trade> findByStatusNative(@Param("status") String status, Pageable pageable);
// countQuery is required for Page return type with native queries!
```

@Modifying for UPDATE and DELETE operations:

```java
// Bulk UPDATE — one SQL, no entity loading:
@Modifying
@Query("UPDATE Trade t SET t.status = :newStatus WHERE t.status = :oldStatus AND t.accountId = :accountId")
int updateStatusForAccount(@Param("oldStatus") String oldStatus,
                           @Param("newStatus") String newStatus,
                           @Param("accountId") String accountId);
// Returns int: number of rows affected

// Bulk DELETE:
@Modifying
@Query("DELETE FROM Trade t WHERE t.status = 'CANCELLED' AND t.accountId = :accountId")
int deleteCancelledForAccount(@Param("accountId") String accountId);

// (Caution) @Modifying requires @Transactional on the calling service method!
// (Caution) After @Modifying, the persistence context may be stale — add clearAutomatically:
@Modifying(clearAutomatically = true)
@Query("UPDATE Trade t SET t.status = 'SETTLED' WHERE t.tradeId = :id")
int settleTrade(@Param("id") String tradeId);
// clearAutomatically=true clears persistence context after update → prevents stale reads
```

